<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tian Shan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tianshan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Shan, the 'Heavenly Mountains', is a great mountain system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> stretching about 2,500 km from Kyrgyzstan and Kazakhstan east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. Its northern and southern foothills held chains of oases and pastures used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and by nomadic powers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>. After 840 Uyghur groups settled the eastern Tian Shan oases around Qocho.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tian-shan.jpg" alt="Tian Shan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maryliflower · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpine_meadows_and_snow-covered_peaks_in_Khan_Tengri_Nature_Park,_Tien_Shan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
